  "grammar_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Talking About the Past ('yakati')",
      "content": "The verb 'yakati' expresses a completed past action. Shona has two main ways to talk about the past: 1) For actions that happened earlier today or very recently, combine the short subject prefix ('nda-' = I, 'wa-' = you, 'a-' = he/she, 'ta-' = we, 'va-' = they) directly with the verb stem (e.g., 'ndamuka' = 'I woke up today', 'wabudirira' = 'you have succeeded'). 2) For events that happened yesterday or further in the past, insert '-ka-' after the subject prefix (e.g., 'takatenga' = 'we bought', 'vakaenda' = 'they went', 'akadanidzira' = 'he shouted').",
      "rule": "Recent Today Past: [nda- / wa- / ta- / va-] + verb | Earlier Past: [ndaka- / waka- / taka- / vaka-] + verb"
    },
    {
      "title": "Colours & Describing Objects in Shona ('cheruvara')",
      "content": "This passage uses colour vocabulary such as 'cheruvara'. Shona has three traditional core colour adjectives that change their prefix slightly to match the noun they describe: '-chena' (white/clean, e.g., 'ruvara ruchena' or 'shangu chena'), '-tema / nhema' (black/dark, e.g., 'mota nhema' or 'ruvara rutema'), and '-tsvuku' (red/bright brown, e.g., 'mota tsvuku' or 'ruvara rutsvuku'). For other colours like green ('girinhi'), blue ('bhuruu'), or orange ('orenji'), speakers typically say 'ruvara rwe-' ('the colour of...') followed by the colour name, as in 'ruvara rwegirinhi' (green).",
      "rule": "Core Colours: -chena (white), -tema / nhema (black), -tsvuku (red) | Phrase pattern: ruvara rwe-[colour] (e.g., ruvara rwegirinhi)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Joining Words with 'ne-' ('and / with') in 'nechikafu'",
      "content": "Look closely at 'nechikafu'. In Shona, the word for 'and' or 'with' between nouns is not a separate word—instead, the prefix 'ne-' (or 'na-' before people's names) is written attached directly to the second noun! For example, 'ne-' + 'chikafu' forms 'nechikafu'. You will see this everywhere in daily speech: 'sadza nemuriwo' ('sadza and vegetables'), 'nesimba' ('with strength'), and 'nekukasira' ('with haste / quickly').",
      "rule": "ne- + Noun = and / with [Noun] (written as a single word, e.g., sadza nemuriwo = sadza and vegetables)"
    }
  ],
  "cultural_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Generous Hospitality ('Ndiro Yakati Tukutuku') with Rice, Gravy & Braised Meat",
      "content": "Notice the expressive ideophone 'tukutuku' in 'ndiro yakati tukutuku nechikafu'—meaning a plate heaped generously to the brim! Serving rice ('mupunga') steeped in rich gravy ('muto') with boiled-then-fried meat ('nyama yakakwatiswa ikakangwa') is a classic Sunday and festive meal.",
      "rule": null
    }
  ],
